  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1202 AM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nTuesday, we will see a better chance of showers and isolated thunder \nover the mtns. On this day, a weak disturbance will cross the region \nand this will allow for some upward lift to move across the region \nassisting in convective development. We may even see some weak \nconvection move over the N El Paso county region. Tuesday will \nlikely be the warmest day of the week, with mid 80s over the plains \nand 70-75F over the valleys. \n\nFrom Wednesday through Saturday, Not much change expected these \ndays. we will see isolated showers/-tsra over the higher terrain in \nthe afternoon. Temps over the plains will generally be in the upper \n70s to lower 80s. with 65-70F in the valleys.\n\nThings start to change Sunday. Statistical guidance indicate temps \nwill decrease several degrees region wide on this day with a better \nchance of showers over the higher terrain. What is uncertain is HOW \nmuch cooler it will be over the region as the variability in the max \ntemp fcst data show a rather large range. This large range in temps \ncontinues into early next week. Additionally, POP chances increase \nsignificantly over the higher terrain early/mid week with scattered \npops over the plains. Perusing the deterministic guidance products \nshows a stout progressive mid level trough swinging across the \nregion during the Tuesday/Wednesday time frame. However, the \ndeterministic guidance products vary in the intensity of the trough \nmoving through. However, at this time it appears that we could see \nsome heavier snow fall amounts over the CONTDVD region during this \ntime period with cooler (much cooler?) temps over the plains. \n\\/Hodanish",
